#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719917 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle0,-1,2\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle1,0,2\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle2,1,-1\\rangle$, find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1&0&2\\\\2&1&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}0&2\\\\1&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}\n-\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\2&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}\n+\\begin{vmatrix}1&0\\\\2&1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=(0(-1)-2(1))\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad -(1(-1)-2(2))\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad +(1(1)-0(2))\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=-2\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=\\langle-2,5,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})\n&=\\langle0,-1,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle-2,5,1\\rangle\\\\\n&=0(-2)+(-1)(5)+2(1)\\\\\n&=-5+2\\\\\n&=-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719923 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the portion of $y=\\ln(1+x^2)$ with $x>0$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=\\ln 2$ and $y=\\ln 5$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(xe^y-y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use the horizontal lines to find the endpoints. Since $x>0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln(1+x^2)=\\ln 2&\\implies 1+x^2=2\\implies x=1,\\\\\n\\ln(1+x^2)=\\ln 5&\\implies 1+x^2=5\\implies x=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe positive branch is increasing, so these intersections bound one section. Parametrize it by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln(1+t^2)\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nApply the chain rule before forming the arc-length factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{2t}{1+t^2}\\right\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2t}{1+t^2}\\right)^2}\n=\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4t^2}{(1+t^2)^2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into $f(x,y)=xe^y-y$. Using $e^{\\ln(1+t^2)}=1+t^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))\n&=t e^{\\ln(1+t^2)}-\\ln(1+t^2)\\\\\n&=t(1+t^2)-\\ln(1+t^2).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, multiply the scalar by the speed and use the $t=x$ bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n=\\int_1^2 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting gives the required definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_1^2 \\left[t(1+t^2)-\\ln(1+t^2)\\right]\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4t^2}{(1+t^2)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719931 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=e^x$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=e$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First find the $x$-coordinates of the intersections:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^x=1&\\implies x=0,\\\\\ne^x=e&\\implies x=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,e^t\\rangle$ for $0\\le t\\le 1$. Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 1,e^t\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+(e^t)^2}=\\sqrt{1+e^{2t}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into $f(x,y)=xy$:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=t e^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 t e^t\\sqrt{1+e^{2t}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe horizontal lines supply $y$-values; they are not the limits when $t=x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719937 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the section of $y=x^2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$, shown below.\n\n![The parabola from (0,0) to (1,1).](images/a8/a8a3fe6c05479258f84aa074398e92a8a849fa4d015feb450aeef6a6066a870e.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$ and $y=t^2$. The parameter interval is $0\\le t\\le 1$, so\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 1,2t\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1^2+(2t)^2}=\\sqrt{1+4t^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar function is $f(x,y)=y$, hence $f(\\mathbf r(t))=t^2$. Combining this with the arc-length factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 t^2\\sqrt{1+4t^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719944 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 3 t,t^{2} + 1\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTraverse $C$ from $t=0$ to $t=1.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(0)&=\\left\\langle 0,1\\right\\rangle, \\\\ \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left\\langle 3,2\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these points into the supplied potential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0,1)&=- 2 \\left(0\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=-2, \\\\ f(3,2)&=- 2 \\left(3\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(2\\right) \\\\ &=-22.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=f(3,2)-f(0,1) \\\\ &=\\left(-22\\right)-\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ &=-20.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719949 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2 y e^{2 x}.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t,- 3 t^{2}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTraverse $C$ from $t=0$ to $t=-1.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(0)&=\\left\\langle 0,0\\right\\rangle, \\\\ \\mathbf r(-1)&=\\left\\langle -1,-3\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these points into the supplied potential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0,0)&=2 \\left(0\\right) e^{2 \\left(0\\right)} \\\\ &=0, \\\\ f(-1,-3)&=2 \\left(-3\\right) e^{2 \\left(-1\\right)} \\\\ &=- \\frac{6}{e^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=f(-1,-3)-f(0,0) \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{6}{e^{2}}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{6}{e^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719956 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},1 - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTraverse $C$ from $t=2 \\pi$ to $t=\\pi.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(2 \\pi)&=\\left\\langle 2 \\pi,0\\right\\rangle, \\\\ \\mathbf r(\\pi)&=\\left\\langle \\pi,2\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these points into the supplied potential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2 \\pi,0)&=2 \\left(2 \\pi\\right)^{2} \\left(0\\right) - 2 \\left(2 \\pi\\right) \\left(0\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=0, \\\\ f(\\pi,2)&=2 \\left(\\pi\\right)^{2} \\left(2\\right) - 2 \\left(\\pi\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=- 8 \\pi + 4 \\pi^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=f(\\pi,2)-f(2 \\pi,0) \\\\ &=\\left(- 8 \\pi + 4 \\pi^{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=- 8 \\pi + 4 \\pi^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719957 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- x^{3} - y.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 3 t,t^{3}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTraverse $C$ from $t=-1$ to $t=1.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(-1)&=\\left\\langle -3,-1\\right\\rangle, \\\\ \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left\\langle 3,1\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these points into the supplied potential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-3,-1)&=- \\left(-3\\right)^{3} - \\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &=28, \\\\ f(3,1)&=- \\left(3\\right)^{3} - \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=-28.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=f(3,1)-f(-3,-1) \\\\ &=\\left(-28\\right)-\\left(28\\right) \\\\ &=-56.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719970 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x^{2} y e^{x y}.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTraverse $C$ from $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(0)&=\\left\\langle 3,0\\right\\rangle, \\\\ \\mathbf r\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)&=\\left\\langle 0,4\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these points into the supplied potential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(3,0)&=\\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(0\\right) e^{\\left(3\\right) \\left(0\\right)} \\\\ &=0, \\\\ f(0,4)&=\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\left(4\\right) e^{\\left(0\\right) \\left(4\\right)} \\\\ &=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=f(0,4)-f(3,0) \\\\ &=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719976 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral ABCD with a dashed enclosing edge](images/bb/bbff14d8535244160e8a241dd6193db7ee56c04083205d84c1854fa3b35d120d.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $83\\,\\text{in}^{2}$. The sides $AB=16\\,\\text{in}$ and $AD=18\\,\\text{in}$ meet at $57^\\circ$. What is the area of $\\triangle BCD$, rounded to the **nearest integer**?\n\nA scientific calculator is required." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $ABD$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{ABD}&=\\frac12\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot\\sin\\angle BAD\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 16\\cdot 18\\cdot\\sin57^\\circ\\\\&\\approx120.768562\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ and the missing triangle $BCD$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{BCD}&=A_{ABD}-A_{ABCD}\\\\&=144\\sin57^\\circ-83\\\\&\\approx37.768562\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $38\\,\\text{in}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719984 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral BCDA with a dashed enclosing edge](images/2c/2c1180d2676b37e7c91cdd1f11d2fc3a8a583d667b26ffd5131bca4ac6bd5c13.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $BCDA$ is $8\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$. The sides $BC=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $BA=6\\,\\text{cm}$ meet at $75^\\circ$. What is the area of $\\triangle CDA$, rounded to the **nearest integer**?\n\nA scientific calculator is required." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $BCA$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{BCA}&=\\frac12\\cdot BC\\cdot BA\\cdot\\sin\\angle CBA\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 4\\cdot 6\\cdot\\sin75^\\circ\\\\&\\approx11.591110\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $BCDA$ and the missing triangle $CDA$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{CDA}&=A_{BCA}-A_{BCDA}\\\\&=12\\sin75^\\circ-8\\\\&\\approx3.591110\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $4\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719985 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral DABC with a dashed enclosing edge](images/cb/cbc857d026fb2afe0a1a9f643d42578ca4e5b301a4a7a6367272a932ab014d42.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $DABC$ is $4\\,\\text{in}^{2}$. The sides $DA=8\\,\\text{in}$ and $DC=6\\,\\text{in}$ meet at $42^\\circ$. What is the area of $\\triangle ABC$, rounded to the **nearest integer**?\n\nA scientific calculator is required." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $DAC$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{DAC}&=\\frac12\\cdot DA\\cdot DC\\cdot\\sin\\angle ADC\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 8\\cdot 6\\cdot\\sin42^\\circ\\\\&\\approx16.059135\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $DABC$ and the missing triangle $ABC$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{ABC}&=A_{DAC}-A_{DABC}\\\\&=24\\sin42^\\circ-4\\\\&\\approx12.059135\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $12\\,\\text{in}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719991 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral DABC with a dashed enclosing edge](images/42/42ad0f671e1fe52287c2c03031b292078d7ad4980d4bf2e929cc5e45ca7f9830.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $DABC$ is $10\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$. The sides $DA=12\\,\\text{cm}$ and $DC=6\\,\\text{cm}$ meet at $96^\\circ$. The area of $\\triangle ABC$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is {{field-1}} $\\text{cm}^{2}$.\n\nA scientific calculator is required." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $DAC$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{DAC}&=\\frac12\\cdot DA\\cdot DC\\cdot\\sin\\angle ADC\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 12\\cdot 6\\cdot\\sin96^\\circ\\\\&\\approx35.802788\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $DABC$ and the missing triangle $ABC$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{ABC}&=A_{DAC}-A_{DABC}\\\\&=36\\sin96^\\circ-10\\\\&\\approx25.802788\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $26\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720005 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral CDAB with a dashed enclosing edge](images/2b/2b26b4f0c1a7578f3c17534ddca47ad8b297c62807a73938b089fe00c7ec9e29.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $CDAB$ is $11\\,\\text{in}^{2}$. The sides $CD=6\\,\\text{in}$ and $CB=8\\,\\text{in}$ meet at $124^\\circ$. What is the area of $\\triangle DAB$, rounded to the **nearest integer**?\n\nA scientific calculator is required." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $CDB$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{CDB}&=\\frac12\\cdot CD\\cdot CB\\cdot\\sin\\angle DCB\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 6\\cdot 8\\cdot\\sin124^\\circ\\\\&\\approx19.896902\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $CDAB$ and the missing triangle $DAB$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{DAB}&=A_{CDB}-A_{CDAB}\\\\&=24\\sin124^\\circ-11\\\\&\\approx8.896902\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $9\\,\\text{in}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720008 :question/problem "Given $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle-2,0,0\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle-2,-1,3\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle1,1,1\\rangle$, find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, compute the ordered cross product $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\-2&-1&3\\\\1&1&1\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts components are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_1&=(-1)\\cdot1-3\\cdot1=-4 \\\\\nd_2&=-((-2)\\cdot1-3\\cdot1)=5 \\\\\nd_3&=(-2)\\cdot1-(-1)\\cdot1=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}=\\langle-4,5,-1\\rangle$. Now take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})&=\\langle-2,0,0\\rangle\\cdot\\langle-4,5,-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=(-2)\\cdot(-4)+0\\cdot5+0\\cdot(-1) \\\\\n&=8+0+0 \\\\\n&=8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720011 :question/problem "Given $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle-1,3,-2\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle1,0,2\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle2,-3,-2\\rangle$, find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, compute the ordered cross product $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1&0&2\\\\2&-3&-2\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts components are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_1&=0\\cdot(-2)-2\\cdot(-3)=6 \\\\\nd_2&=-(1\\cdot(-2)-2\\cdot2)=6 \\\\\nd_3&=1\\cdot(-3)-0\\cdot2=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}=\\langle6,6,-3\\rangle$. Now take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})&=\\langle-1,3,-2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle6,6,-3\\rangle \\\\\n&=(-1)\\cdot6+3\\cdot6+(-2)\\cdot(-3) \\\\\n&=(-6)+18+6 \\\\\n&=18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720018 :question/problem "Given $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle2,1,-3\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle-2,-2,-2\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle0,3,0\\rangle$, find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, compute the ordered cross product $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\-2&-2&-2\\\\0&3&0\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts components are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_1&=(-2)\\cdot0-(-2)\\cdot3=6 \\\\\nd_2&=-((-2)\\cdot0-(-2)\\cdot0)=0 \\\\\nd_3&=(-2)\\cdot3-(-2)\\cdot0=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}=\\langle6,0,-6\\rangle$. Now take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})&=\\langle2,1,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle6,0,-6\\rangle \\\\\n&=2\\cdot6+1\\cdot0+(-3)\\cdot(-6) \\\\\n&=12+0+18 \\\\\n&=30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720022 :question/problem "Given $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle3,3,2\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle3,-1,3\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle0,-3,-2\\rangle$, find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, compute the ordered cross product $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\3&-1&3\\\\0&-3&-2\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts components are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_1&=(-1)\\cdot(-2)-3\\cdot(-3)=11 \\\\\nd_2&=-(3\\cdot(-2)-3\\cdot0)=6 \\\\\nd_3&=3\\cdot(-3)-(-1)\\cdot0=-9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}=\\langle11,6,-9\\rangle$. Now take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})&=\\langle3,3,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle11,6,-9\\rangle \\\\\n&=3\\cdot11+3\\cdot6+2\\cdot(-9) \\\\\n&=33+18+(-18) \\\\\n&=33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720029 :question/problem "Given $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle1,-1,1\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle-2,2,-3\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle1,-3,2\\rangle$, find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, compute the ordered cross product $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\-2&2&-3\\\\1&-3&2\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts components are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_1&=2\\cdot2-(-3)\\cdot(-3)=-5 \\\\\nd_2&=-((-2)\\cdot2-(-3)\\cdot1)=1 \\\\\nd_3&=(-2)\\cdot(-3)-2\\cdot1=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}=\\langle-5,1,4\\rangle$. Now take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})&=\\langle1,-1,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle-5,1,4\\rangle \\\\\n&=1\\cdot(-5)+(-1)\\cdot1+1\\cdot4 \\\\\n&=(-5)+(-1)+4 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720034 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle0,2,1\\rangle$, $\\mathbf b=\\langle1,0,2\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf c=\\langle2,1,3\\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf a\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)$ using a determinant. Use $\\mathbf a$, $\\mathbf b$, and $\\mathbf c$, in that order, as its rows." :question/worked-solution "The rows follow the order in $\\mathbf a\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)$: $\\mathbf a$, $\\mathbf b$, $\\mathbf c$. The scalar triple product equals the determinant of these rows.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row using the cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=0-2(1\\cdot3-2\\cdot2)+(1\\cdot1-0\\cdot2) \\\\ &=0+2+1 \\\\ &=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720035 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf p=\\langle1,-2,2\\rangle$, $\\mathbf q=\\langle-2,1,3\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf r=\\langle2,3,-1\\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf r\\cdot(\\mathbf q\\times\\mathbf p)$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf r\\cdot(\\mathbf q\\times\\mathbf p)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The rows follow the order in $\\mathbf r\\cdot(\\mathbf q\\times\\mathbf p)$: $\\mathbf r$, $\\mathbf q$, $\\mathbf p$. The scalar triple product equals the determinant of these rows.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r\\cdot(\\mathbf q\\times\\mathbf p)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row using the cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=2(1\\cdot2-3(-2)) \\\\ &\\quad -3((-2)\\cdot2-3\\cdot1) \\\\ &\\quad +(-1)((-2)(-2)-1\\cdot1) \\\\ &=16+21-3 \\\\ &=34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720040 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf p=\\langle1,2,1\\rangle$, $\\mathbf q=\\langle2,-1,0\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf r=\\langle-1,1,2\\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf q\\cdot(\\mathbf p\\times\\mathbf r)$ using a determinant." :question/worked-solution "The rows follow the order in $\\mathbf q\\cdot(\\mathbf p\\times\\mathbf r)$: $\\mathbf q$, $\\mathbf p$, $\\mathbf r$. The scalar triple product equals the determinant of these rows.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf q\\cdot(\\mathbf p\\times\\mathbf r)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row using the cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=2(2\\cdot2-1\\cdot1)-(-1)(1\\cdot2-1(-1))+0 \\\\ &=6+3+0 \\\\ &=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720055 :question/problem "Suppose\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf{b})\\times(-\\mathbf{c})=\\langle4,-6,2\\rangle,\n\\qquad \\mathbf{a}=\\langle1,2,-1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFind $(2\\mathbf{b})\\cdot\\bigl((3\\mathbf{a})\\times(-\\mathbf{c})\\bigr)$.\n\n$(2\\mathbf{b})\\cdot\\bigl((3\\mathbf{a})\\times(-\\mathbf{c})\\bigr)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied cross product; no individual coordinates for $\\mathbf{b}$ or $\\mathbf{c}$ are needed. First take its dot product with $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\bigl((2\\mathbf{b})\\times(-\\mathbf{c})\\bigr)\n&=\\langle1,2,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle4,-6,2\\rangle\\\\\n&=1(4)+2(-6)+(-1)(2)\\\\\n&=-10.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $T=\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})$. Factoring out the two scalars gives\n\n$$\n-2T=-10,\\qquad T=5.\n$$\n\nSwapping $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ changes the sign:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}\\cdot(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{c})=-T=-5.\n$$\n\nNow factor the scalars out of the requested product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\mathbf{b})\\cdot\\bigl((3\\mathbf{a})\\times(-\\mathbf{c})\\bigr)\n&=2(3)(-1)\\bigl[\\mathbf{b}\\cdot(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{c})\\bigr]\\\\\n&=(-6)(-5)\\\\\n&=30.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $2\\mathbf{b}$ in the computed product $-10$, then multiply the new outer vector $\\mathbf{a}$ by $3$: $-3(-10)=30$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720057 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b})$, given\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf{a})\\cdot\\bigl((-3\\mathbf{b})\\times\\mathbf{c}\\bigr)=9.\n$$\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\cdot(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b})=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\mathbf{a})\\cdot\\bigl((-3\\mathbf{b})\\times\\mathbf{c}\\bigr)\n&=2(-3)\\bigl[\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})\\bigr]=9,\\\\\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})&=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSwap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$, then swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. Each swap changes the sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{a})&=\\frac{3}{2},\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}\\cdot(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b})&=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two sign changes cancel, so the requested product is $-\\frac{3}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720058 :question/problem "Given $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})=-6$, find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{c})$." :question/worked-solution "Swapping exactly two vectors in a scalar triple product changes its sign. Swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\cdot(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{c})\n&=-\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})\\\\\n&=-(-6)\\\\\n&=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720079 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y^{2} + y$. Let $C$ follow the curve $x=\\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 1$ as $y$ goes from $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to $- \\pi$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 1,t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to $- \\pi$. The initial point is $A=\\left(-1,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(-2,- \\pi\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=\\left(- \\pi\\right)^{2} + \\left(- \\pi\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\pi + \\pi^{2}\\\\\nf(A)&=\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(- \\pi + \\pi^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2} + \\frac{3 \\pi^{2}}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720081 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x - y + e^{y} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$. Let $C$ follow the curve $y=2x$ as $y$ goes from $0$ to $\\pi$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{t}{2},t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $0$ to $\\pi$. The initial point is $A=\\left(0,0\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) - \\left(\\pi\\right) + e^{\\left(\\pi\\right)} \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{2} + e^{\\pi}\\\\\nf(A)&=\\left(0\\right) - \\left(0\\right) + e^{\\left(0\\right)} \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2} + e^{\\pi}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{2} + e^{\\pi}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720085 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + y^{2} + y$. Let $C$ follow the curve $y^2=4\\left(x - 1\\right)$ as $y$ goes from $0$ to $2$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{t^{2}}{4} + 1,t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $0$ to $2$. The initial point is $A=\\left(1,0\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(2,2\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=- \\left(2\\right)^{2} + \\left(2\\right)^{2} + \\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=2\\\\\nf(A)&=- \\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\n&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720088 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- x - y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$. Let $C$ follow the curve $2x=\\pi y^3$ as $y$ goes from $-1$ to $0$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{\\pi t^{3}}{2},t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $-1$ to $0$. The initial point is $A=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2},-1\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(0,0\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=- \\left(0\\right) - \\left(0\\right) + \\cos{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\\\\n&=1\\\\\nf(A)&=- \\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) - \\left(-1\\right) + \\cos{\\left(\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(-1\\right) \\right)}\\\\\n&=1 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(1 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720089 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x^{2} - x - y + e^{x y}$. Let $C$ follow the curve $\\frac{x + 1}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ as $y$ goes from $4$ to $1$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 2 \\sqrt{t} - 1,t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $4$ to $1$. The initial point is $A=\\left(3,4\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(1,1\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=\\left(1\\right)^{2} - \\left(1\\right) - \\left(1\\right) + e^{\\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)}\\\\\n&=-1 + e\\\\\nf(A)&=\\left(3\\right)^{2} - \\left(3\\right) - \\left(4\\right) + e^{\\left(3\\right) \\left(4\\right)}\\\\\n&=2 + e^{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(-1 + e\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(2 + e^{12}\\right)\\\\\n&=- e^{12} - 3 + e.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720105 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2 x y - 2 y^{2}$. Let $C$ be the part of the curve $y^2=x-1$ lying below the $x$-axis, traversed from its intersection with $x=10$ to its intersection with $x=2$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The specified half-plane selects the negative values of $y$. Solving the curve equation at the two boundary lines gives the directed endpoints below. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^{2} + 1,t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $-3$ to $-1$. The initial point is $A=\\left(10,-3\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(2,-1\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=2 \\left(2\\right) \\left(-1\\right) - 2 \\left(-1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=-6\\\\\nf(A)&=2 \\left(10\\right) \\left(-3\\right) - 2 \\left(-3\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=-78\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(-6\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(-78\\right)\\\\\n&=72.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720110 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + 2 x y$. Let $C$ be the part of the curve $x^2=y-1$ lying to the left of the $y$-axis, traversed from its intersection with $y=5$ to its intersection with $y=2$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The specified half-plane selects the negative values of $x$. Solving the curve equation at the two boundary lines gives the directed endpoints below. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t,t^{2} + 1\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $-2$ to $-1$. The initial point is $A=\\left(-2,5\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(-1,2\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=- \\left(-1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(-1\\right) \\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=-5\\\\\nf(A)&=- \\left(-2\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(-2\\right) \\left(5\\right)\\\\\n&=-24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(-5\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(-24\\right)\\\\\n&=19.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720111 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- 2 x y + 2 y^{2}$. Let $C$ be the part of the curve $y^2=x+2$ lying above the $x$-axis, traversed from its intersection with $x=-1$ to its intersection with $x=2$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The specified half-plane selects the positive values of $y$. Solving the curve equation at the two boundary lines gives the directed endpoints below. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^{2} - 2,t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $1$ to $2$. The initial point is $A=\\left(-1,1\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(2,2\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=- 2 \\left(2\\right) \\left(2\\right) + 2 \\left(2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=0\\\\\nf(A)&=- 2 \\left(-1\\right) \\left(1\\right) + 2 \\left(1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=-4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720121 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + 2 x y$. Let $C$ be the part of the curve $x^2=y$ lying to the left of the $y$-axis, traversed from its intersection with $y=4$ to its intersection with $y=9$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The specified half-plane selects the negative values of $x$. Solving the curve equation at the two boundary lines gives the directed endpoints below. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t,t^{2}\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $-2$ to $-3$. The initial point is $A=\\left(-2,4\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(-3,9\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=- \\left(-3\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(-3\\right) \\left(9\\right)\\\\\n&=-63\\\\\nf(A)&=- \\left(-2\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(-2\\right) \\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(-63\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(-20\\right)\\\\\n&=-43.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720126 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,1,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,1,2\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "First compute the cross product using a determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1&1&0\\\\0&1&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(1\\cdot2-0\\cdot1)\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad-(1\\cdot2-0\\cdot0)\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad+(1\\cdot1-1\\cdot0)\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}=\\langle 2,-2,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude of this cross product is the parallelogram's area:\n\n$$\nA=\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\|=\\sqrt{2^2+(-2)^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\nThe lesson diagram illustrates this relationship; its arrows show general vectors.\n\n![The cross-product magnitude equals the area of the parallelogram spanned by two vectors.](images/27/2717ed1209f60dac12c3fcb07aea25f99c49b053fe0ce440b7ce996002a067c7.png)\n\nWe do not halve the magnitude here because the requested shape is a parallelogram."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720130 :question/problem "The triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle x,1,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,1,2\\rangle$ has area $\\sqrt{6}$. Given that $x>0$, find $x$.\n\n$x=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product in terms of the unknown component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\x&1&0\\\\0&1&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(1\\cdot2-0\\cdot1)\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad-(x\\cdot2-0\\cdot0)\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad+(x\\cdot1-1\\cdot0)\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=2\\mathbf{i}-2x\\mathbf{j}+x\\mathbf{k}=\\langle 2,-2x,x\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is the area of the parallelogram, which is twice the given triangle area. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac12\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\|&=\\sqrt{6}\\\\\n\\frac12\\sqrt{2^2+(-2x)^2+x^2}&=\\sqrt{6}\\\\\n\\sqrt{4+5x^2}&=2\\sqrt{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth sides are nonnegative. Squaring and solving gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4+5x^2&=24\\\\\n5x^2&=20\\\\\nx^2&=4\\\\\nx&=\\pm2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x>0$, the required value is $x=2$. Checking this in the original area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\frac12\\sqrt{4+5(2)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{24}}2=\\sqrt{6},\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with the given triangle area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720135 :question/problem "Calculate the area $A_{\\triangle}$ of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-1,2\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 2,1,0\\rangle$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$A_{\\triangle}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1&-1&2\\\\2&1&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((-1)\\cdot0-2\\cdot1)\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad-(1\\cdot0-2\\cdot2)\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad+(1\\cdot1-(-1)\\cdot2)\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=-2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}=\\langle -2,4,3\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding parallelogram has area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{\\mathrm{parallelogram}}&=\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\|\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+4^2+3^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{29}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle is half of that parallelogram, so\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle}=\\frac12\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\|=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720143 :question/problem "Evaluate the scalar line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- \\frac{1}{x - y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is given by $y=x - 1$ for $-2\\le x\\le -1$." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$ and use the explicit equation to parametrize the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad -2\\le t\\le -1\n$$\n\nDifferentiate and calculate the nonnegative speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle1,1\\rangle\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(1\\right)^2}=\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=f\\left(t,t - 1\\right)=-1\n$$\n\nInclude the speed factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{-2}^{-1} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\left(-1\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the product before integrating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\left(- \\sqrt{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[- \\sqrt{2} t\\right]_{-2}^{-1}\\\\\n&=\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)-\\left(2 \\sqrt{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720147 :question/problem "Evaluate the scalar line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is given by $y=2 x - 1$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$ and use the explicit equation to parametrize the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad -1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nDifferentiate and calculate the nonnegative speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle1,2\\rangle\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(2\\right)^2}=\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=f\\left(t,2 t - 1\\right)=3 t - 1\n$$\n\nInclude the speed factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{-1}^{2} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(3 t - 1\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the product before integrating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(\\sqrt{5} \\left(3 t - 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[\\sqrt{5} \\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} - t\\right)\\right]_{-1}^{2}\\\\\n&=\\left(4 \\sqrt{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720151 :question/problem "Evaluate the scalar line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x \\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is given by $y=3 x - 2$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$ and use the explicit equation to parametrize the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,3 t - 2\\rangle,\\qquad -1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nDifferentiate and calculate the nonnegative speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle1,3\\rangle\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(3\\right)^2}=\\sqrt{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=f\\left(t,3 t - 2\\right)=- 3 t^{2}\n$$\n\nInclude the speed factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{-1}^{2} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(- 3 t^{2}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{10}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the product before integrating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(- 3 \\sqrt{10} t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[- \\sqrt{10} t^{3}\\right]_{-1}^{2}\\\\\n&=\\left(- 8 \\sqrt{10}\\right)-\\left(\\sqrt{10}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 9 \\sqrt{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
